> Rob,
> termination, absence thereof, may result in intermittent problems.
> I've seen VAXstation 4000's and microVAX 3100's fitted only with
> internal SCSI devices
> (disks and a CDrom drive) running smoothly without a terminator on the
> back of the box.
> Attach an expansion box and  suddenly the terminator becomes
> necessary.
> Mixing devices adhering to various SCSI versions on one bus never was
> a problem.
> One system, a VAXststion 4000 90A has an RZ25L, an SCA disk with
> converter and a
> 68 pin fast wide SCSI disk with converter and the ensemble runs
> flawless.
> You did not specify the external SCSI device, more specifically the
> length of the connecting
> cable and the storage cabinet used. Internal wiring may present a
> longer timing delay than external
> cables so bus timing may be an issue.
> Last possibility, the external device does not play according to the
> rules.
> One device is bus master and may transmit data. No tagged command
> queuing, which is why VAX SCSI clusters
> don't work. But on a SCSI cluster with VAX hosts you'll see exactly
> the same behaviour as you reported.
> Hans

