geezer wrote:
> On Mar 4, 12:02 am, Don North <idontw...@nospam.com> wrote:
>> geezer wrote:
>>> Anyone know what devices these boot ROMS are for?
>>> 786A9, 787A9, and 788A9.
>>> Brian.
>> I have a .bin image of these. The device code is 'XM' and
>> the default CSR address is 760010. They appear to be an
>> earlier rev of the DMC11 DECNET boot PROMs (released revs
>> were ultimately 862A9 thru 864A9). Code is very similar
>> to these PROMs.
>>
>> Don
> 
> If these are indeed DECnet ROMS,  could I boot RSTS or RSX from a
> Linux box?

Well, sort of, but there are a lot of gotchas:

1) These boot PROMs are for a DMC11, which probably would use some
form of high speed serial line (9600baud to 1.5Mbit) as the interface,
so you would need both a real DMC11 as well as the h/w on the other
end (ie, in your Linux box).

2) You would need DECNET s/w on your Linux box, or at least enough
of DECNET to field boot requests.

3) You need DECNET bootable images of RSTS and/or RSX.

None of these are insurmountable given enough time, money, and
persistence, but this is not a project for the faint of heart,
and in the end probably not worth the effort at all just to be
able to make use of these PROMs...

Don


