> but the tape release flap is jammed closed and will not open. . .

If the tape leader has fallen off, or there is sufficient dust buildup
on the LED's that are "looking across" the tape path are dirty,
the drive might not want to let you open the door.

Pop the three screws off the metal shield that covers the tape path.
(Pay attention to where they go -- they're not all the same length.)

Take some canned air, and blow out the drive, including
the boards down below.    Check the tape leader, to make sure
that it hasn't broken, or fallen off the little clip that holds it towards
the front of the drive.   Take some alcohol or suitable cleaning fluid,
and clean off both of the LED's.

Carefully pull up on the tape head, and clean it as well;
both the center metal core, and the tan plastic parts.

If you're using a "tech swab", be careful not to soak it
too much with cleaning fluid.  The tan plastic guides
are glued on, and will actually fall off if enough solvent
gets in there. . .

Make sure the white plastic tape hub (the part towards
the front of the drive that interfaces with the tape cartridge itself
turns with relatively little effort.  I've had some of those gum up on me.

Re-install the top shield, and try powering up the drive.

If the red light still flashes, try pressing-and-releasing the
button several times, in rapid succession, finally leaving
it in the out / unload position.

Still a no-go?   Reach around to the front, right side
of the drive.   There is a little solenoid there that
holds the door release.    Make sure it's not stuck.
Move it manually, and then you can lift the door handle.



>
> Yes, TK70 needs a M7559 while a M7546 is for TK50.
>
>

You can attach a TK50 drive to a TQK70 controller (M7559),
and it will work just fine as a TK50. . .

This is documented on-line, and, despite the warnings on TV,
I did indeed "try this at home".   ;-)    It works fine.

I have not tried a TK70 on a M7546 though.

However, since the TK70 will read TK50 media,
it certainly couldn't hurt to plug it in, and see if it works.

The data bus is clearly identical, so nothing is going to go up in smoke.




